  "Question": "The fraction \\[ \\frac{2(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6)}{3\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}} \\] is equal to",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{2\\sqrt2}{3}\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac43\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{16}{9}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The nested radical suggests checking whether \\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6\\) is a multiple of \\(\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}\\). Squaring the sum reveals that relationship:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6)^2\n=2+6+2\\sqrt{12}\n=8+4\\sqrt3\n=4(2+\\sqrt3).\n\\]\nBoth radical expressions are positive, so taking positive square roots gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6=2\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is positive, so cancellation is valid:\n\\[\n\\frac{2(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6)}{3\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}}\n=\\frac{4\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}}{3\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}}\n=\\frac43.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac43}\\), choice D.",
